# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Tool write-ups (description field) for Constructions and Tilings. Keyed by tool media id."""
DESCRIPTIONS = {
"M1.S2.T2.C6.2D1": (
"A segment XY of 6 cm lies across a square grid. Beside it sits a loose point P with two live readings, PX and PY, written in centimetres. "
"Under the grid are a Drop button, a count of the dots dropped so far, and a Show Line switch that stays dim at first. "
"Nothing else is drawn: the line this activity is about stays hidden until the student has found its points.\n\n"
"The student drags P around the grid and watches the two readings change. When PX and PY agree, both turn green and Drop works, leaving a dot where P stands. "
"When they differ, Drop is refused and both numbers flash red, with a note saying which end of XY the point is nearer to. "
"The student collects at least five dots, some above XY and some below it. Once five dots are down, the Show Line switch lights up. "
"Before touching it, the student predicts the shape the dots make. Switching it on draws one straight line through all of them. "
"It meets XY at its midpoint, and a right angle mark flashes there with the angle named as 90 degrees.\n\n"
"The six cards ask for: five equal-distance dots on the 6 cm segment; the one equal-distance point that lies on XY itself, which is the midpoint, 3 cm from each end; "
"two points that are 5 cm from both X and Y, one on each side, which sit 4 cm from the midpoint and are the centres A and B of the eye; "
"the same search with XY turned slanting, where the dots and the line turn with it; "
"a test of a point 4 cm from X and 5 cm from Y, which is refused as being off the line and marked as nearer X; "
"and naming the angle between the line and XY, which is 90 degrees.\n\n"
"Hints come in order. First: what must be true of PX and PY for a dot to be accepted? "
"Then: find the midpoint first, then look for equal-distance points straight above and below it. "
"Then: keep P on the line through the midpoint at a right angle to XY and watch the two distances. "
"Last, the tool states that all equal-distance points lie on the perpendicular bisector, through the midpoint and at 90 degrees to XY. "
"Through doing, the student learns that the points equally far from X and Y are not scattered but all lie on one line, the perpendicular bisector of XY, and that the midpoint is only one of them."
),

"M1.S4.T4.C10.2D1": (
"On the bench lies a segment AB of 5 cm with its length written beside it. Below it sit a virtual compass with a radius slider that reads in centimetres, "
"and an unmarked ruler. A check panel is empty at first. Once a line is drawn it shows the angle the line makes with AB and the lengths of the two parts it cuts AB into.\n\n"
"The student sets a radius, taps A to draw an arc, taps B to draw an arc of the same radius, and sees whether the arcs cross. "
"Where they cross, a dot appears above AB and another below it. The ruler then joins the two dots and the check panel fills in. "
"With a radius of 4 cm the arcs cross, the line cuts AB into 2.5 cm and 2.5 cm, the 90 degree mark appears and the panel turns green. "
"First the student predicts how small the radius can go. At 2 cm the two arcs fall short of each other, with the 1 cm gap highlighted. "
"At 2.5 cm, half of AB, they only touch, so there is one point where two are needed. So the radius has to be more than half of AB.\n\n"
"The student then breaks the other rule on purpose. An arc of 4 cm from A and an arc of 3 cm from B still cross, "
"but the line through the crossings cuts AB at 3.2 cm from A and 1.8 cm from B, so the panel shows unequal parts and stays red. "
"The two arcs of one pair must have the same radius. In the last card two different pairs are used, a pair of 4 cm arcs and a pair of 6 cm arcs, "
"to mark points that are 4 cm and 6 cm from both A and B. The line through those two points is the same bisector, so different pairs may use different radii. "
"The six cards are AB of 5 cm, the 2 cm radius, AB of 9.5 cm (the midpoint is found at 4.75 cm), AB slanting at 30 degrees, the 4 cm and 3 cm test, and the 4 cm and 6 cm points.\n\n"
"Hints come in order. First: what happens to the arcs if the radius is very small? Then: the radius must be more than half of AB, and the same from A and from B. "
"Then: for AB of 5 cm try a radius of 4 cm from both ends, then join the crossings. "
"Last: both arcs of radius more than half AB cross at two points, and their line cuts AB in half at 90 degrees. "
"Through doing, the student learns which choices in the construction matter and which do not, and that any two points that are each equally far from A and B fix the perpendicular bisector."
),

"M2.S8.T8.C19.2D1": (
"An angle opens on the screen with a size slider beneath it, set to 90 degrees. A virtual compass and an unmarked ruler are in the tray. "
"At the side a step list waits with three unticked lines: arc from the vertex, arcs from the two arm points, join to the vertex. "
"A readout for the two halves is blank, and a petal canvas stands empty beside the angle.\n\n"
"The student draws an arc centred on the vertex O that cuts the two arms at A and B, so that OA equals OB. "
"Then, with one radius, they draw an arc from A and an arc from B and mark the point C where they cross. Each finished step ticks itself off. "
"Joining O to C splits the angle, and the readout shows both halves, 45 degrees each for the 90 degree start. "
"The triangles OAC and OBC are shaded to show that their three sides match in pairs, which is the SSS reason. "
"If the student uses different radii from A and from B, the crossing point is wrong and the halves appear in red with unequal readings. "
"The student then halves the 45 degrees again and gets 22.5 degrees.\n\n"
"The six cards ask the student to bisect 90 degrees for 45 degrees; bisect 120 degrees for 60 degrees; bisect 70 degrees and check that both halves read 35 degrees; "
"make 22.5 degrees by halving twice from 90 degrees; and decide whether 65.5 degrees can be made by halving from 90 degrees and 60 degrees. "
"For that card the tool lists the angles that repeated halving does reach, such as 45, 22.5, 30, 15 and 67.5 degrees, and 65.5 degrees is not on the list. "
"The last card builds the eight-petal design. Eight equal angles share the full turn of 360 degrees, so each is 45 degrees. "
"The student draws two perpendicular lines through a centre and bisects two neighbouring right angles. The four lines now make eight angles of 45 degrees, and the canvas draws the eight-petal design on them.\n\n"
"Hints come in order. First: which two points do the second pair of arcs start from? Then: use one radius from both arm points so the two triangles have three equal sides. "
"Then: for 90 degrees, arcs from both arm points cross on the line that makes 45 degrees with each arm. "
"Last: equal arcs give congruent triangles by SSS, so the line splits the angle into equal halves. "
"Through doing, the student learns why the bisector works, that halving can be repeated, and that halving reaches only certain angles."
),

"M2.S10.T10.C25.2D1": (
"Two separate pieces sit on the board. On the left is a given angle with vertex A, drawn with two arms. "
"On the right is a single ray starting at X, whose end the student can drag to point in any direction: flat, slanting, upward, or the other way. "
"The compass keeps the last radius it was set to, and a step list shows four lines: arc on the given angle, the same arc on the new ray, measure the chord, transfer the chord.\n\n"
"The student draws an arc from A across both arms. It cuts them at B and C and makes the triangle ABC. "
"With the compass untouched, they draw an arc from X that cuts the new ray at Z. "
"Next they open the compass on the chord BC, put its point on Z and cut the arc at Y, then draw the ray from X through Y. "
"No scale appears anywhere, because the length of BC is carried by the compass. "
"A Lift button then slides the new angle on top of the original. When the copy is right the arms land exactly on each other and the triangles ABC and XYZ are shaded as the three-equal-sides pair.\n\n"
"A changed radius on the second arc shows the copy too wide or too narrow, with the arms in red. "
"The student also stretches the arms of the original, one long and one short, and sees that the angle readout does not change, so the angle does not depend on how long the arms are. "
"The six cards ask for a 50 degree angle copied onto a flat ray; a 110 degree angle copied onto a slanting ray; an angle copied onto a ray pointing the other way; "
"an angle copied twice side by side so that the outer angle is double the first; a sector angle copied along a line to start a row of sectors; and an angle with one long arm and one short arm.\n\n"
"Hints come in order. First: which two lengths do you need to carry from the given angle to the new ray? Then: keep the same arc radius on both, then carry the chord length. "
"Then: set the compass on the two arc points of the given angle, then cut the new arc with it. "
"Last: same arc and same chord give congruent triangles by SSS, so the angles are equal. "
"Through doing, the student learns how to copy an angle in any position without measuring, and why the copy is exact."
),

"M2.S12.T12.C29.2D1": (
"A line l runs across the screen and a point P sits off it. Along the edges are a ruler, the arc and chord steps from the angle copier, "
"a gap meter that reads the distance between two lines at both ends of the screen, and a slider. "
"The student starts by drawing any line through P that cuts l. This line is the transversal, and the point where it meets l is the cutting point.\n\n"
"Next the student copies the angle that the transversal makes with l, from the cutting point to P, on the same side. "
"They draw an arc at the cutting point, the same arc at P, take the chord with the compass and carry it across. They then draw the new line n through P. "
"The tool marks the two angles that match, as corresponding angles, and measures the gap between n and l at both ends. "
"Equal gaps turn the meter green. The slider then moves n along the transversal, and the two gap readings change together without ever separating.\n\n"
"If the student copies the angle on the other side of the transversal, the new line leans the other way and meets l, and the crossing point is shown, so the student sees why it is not parallel. "
"Finally the student draws a second, steeper transversal through P, repeats the construction, and finds that the new line lands exactly on the first one, shown overlapping. "
"The five cards are a flat l; a slanting l; P far from l; the wrong-side copy; and the steeper transversal.\n\n"
"Hints come in order. First: which angle at the cutting point should appear again at P? Then: copy the angle between the transversal and l, on the same side, at P. "
"Then: arc at the cutting point, the same arc at P, then carry the chord. "
"Last: equal corresponding angles make the line through P parallel to l. "
"Through doing, the student learns that a parallel line can be drawn without a set square, that any transversal works, and that equal corresponding angles are what make the lines parallel."
),

"M3.S14.T14.C34.2D1": (
"Coloured wedges are fitted one after another round a point O, each labelled with its angle. They do not close the full turn: one gap is left open and drawn in red. "
"At the bottom are a box for the student's gap answer and a slider that sets the angle of a new wedge.\n\n"
"In the first card the wedges are 60, 60, 60, 60 and 70 degrees. The student does not just look. They add the angles that are already there, take the sum away from 360 degrees, and type the gap. "
"The tool checks the numbers: the sum is 310 degrees, so the gap is 50 degrees. "
"The student then sets the slider and tries to place a new wedge. A 70 degree wedge is bigger than the gap, so it overlaps its neighbour and the 20 degree overlap is shaded red. "
"A 40 degree wedge leaves a sliver whose size, 10 degrees, is written on it. A 50 degree wedge snaps in, and the full turn of 360 degrees is marked.\n\n"
"The six cards are: the gap left by 60, 60, 60, 60 and 70 degrees; whether a 70 degree wedge can fit that gap; "
"five 60 degree wedges, where the gap is 60 degrees and one more wedge of that size fits; wedges of 90, 90 and 100 degrees, where the sum is 280 degrees and the gap is 80 degrees; "
"six 60 degree wedges, which close the hexagon exactly; and a last card that shows that AOD is a straight line in that hexagon. "
"After the hexagon closes, the student taps an outer corner and the readout shows 60 plus 60, which is 120 degrees, so a corner of the hexagon is not 60 degrees. "
"For the last card the student picks the three wedges between A and D, and their angles add up to 180 degrees, a straight angle.\n\n"
"Hints come in order. First: how many degrees make a full turn round a point? Then: add the angles already there and take the sum away from 360 degrees. "
"Then: for 60, 60, 60, 60 and 70 degrees the sum is 310 degrees. "
"Last: the gap is 50 degrees, so a 70 degree angle cannot fit; only 50 degrees fits. "
"Through doing, the student learns to find a gap by subtraction from 360 degrees and not by eye, and why six equilateral triangles fit round a point to make a hexagon."
),

"M3.S16.T16.C39.2D1": (
"A ray from a start point A runs across the bench. The compass keeps its radius, a step list ticks off each arc as it is drawn, and an angle readout at the side starts empty. "
"A switch at the top changes the bench between Angle mode and Hexagon mode.\n\n"
"In Angle mode the student draws an arc centred at A that cuts the ray at B. With the same radius they draw an arc centred at B that meets the first arc at C. "
"Joining A to C gives the readout 60 degrees, and the triangle ABC is shaded with its three sides, AB, AC and BC, all shown equal to the radius. "
"To get 120 degrees the student steps the same radius along the first arc once more, from C to a new mark D, and the angle from the ray to AD reads 120 degrees, two 60 degree angles side by side. "
"If the student changes the radius between the two arcs, the triangle is no longer equilateral, the three sides are shown unequal, and the angle goes wrong.\n\n"
"In Hexagon mode a circle is drawn with a radius equal to the side, first 4 cm and then 5 cm. "
"The student sets the compass to the side, walks it six times round the circle from a starting mark, and joins the six marks. "
"With the right radius the sixth mark lands on the start. With a wrong setting it falls short of the start or goes past it. "
"The six cards ask for 60 degrees; 120 degrees; a regular hexagon of side 4 cm; a regular hexagon of side 5 cm; the broken-radius test; "
"and a check that each angle of the finished hexagon reads 120 degrees, because two 60 degree triangle angles meet at every corner.\n\n"
"Hints come in order. First: what kind of triangle has all three sides equal to the compass radius? Then: keep one radius for both arcs so the triangle is equilateral. "
"Then: for 120 degrees, step the same radius along the arc a second time from the first crossing. "
"Last: an equilateral triangle has 60 degree angles; two side by side make 120 degrees, the angle of a regular hexagon. "
"Through doing, the student learns how an equilateral triangle gives 60 degrees, how two of them give 120 degrees, and why the side of a regular hexagon equals the radius of the circle round it."
),

"M3.S18.T18.C45.2D1": (
"A line l runs across the screen and a point P sits above it. In the tray are a compass and a ruler. "
"A step list shows three lines: arc from P cutting l twice, equal arcs from the two cut points, join to P. "
"An angle readout waits at the side, and a mode switch changes the tool between Perpendicular and Angle.\n\n"
"In Perpendicular mode the student draws an arc centred at P that is big enough to cut l at two points. "
"Because both points lie on the same arc, each is the same distance from P, and the tool shows this by drawing the two radii. "
"From the two cut points the student draws arcs of one radius that cross on the far side of l, and joins the crossing to P. "
"The readout shows the angle of the new line with l as 90 degrees, and the two congruent triangles are shaded. "
"An arc that does not reach l shows no cut points and asks for a bigger radius.\n\n"
"The student does the construction for a flat l, then for P close to l, then for a slanting l, and then draws an arc that is too small to reach l and sees what goes wrong. "
"In Angle mode the student builds 60 degrees with two equal arcs, bisects it to get 30 degrees, and bisects 30 degrees to get 15 degrees. "
"The halves read equal, with the SSS triangles shaded each time. "
"The six cards are the flat l, P close to l, the slanting l, the too-small arc, 30 degrees and 15 degrees.\n\n"
"Hints come in order. First: which two points on l are the same distance from P? Then: an arc from P gives them; their perpendicular bisector goes through P. "
"Then: from the two cut points draw equal arcs that cross on the side away from P. "
"Last: join the crossing to P; P is equally far from both cut points, so the line meets l at 90 degrees. "
"Through doing, the student learns how to drop a perpendicular from a point outside a line by finding a segment whose perpendicular bisector passes through the point, and how repeated halving of 60 degrees gives 30 and 15 degrees."
),

"M4.S20.T20.C52.2D1": (
"A blank grid of unit squares fills the board, with one stepper for the number of rows and one for the number of columns. "
"A tray holds vertical and horizontal 2 by 1 tiles that the student drags onto the grid. A counter shows the squares left to cover, "
"and an Impossible button waits at the bottom, asking for a reason when it is pressed.\n\n"
"The student sets 4 rows and 6 columns, predicts how many tiles it will take, and drags tiles into place. "
"A tile that overlaps another tile or runs off the grid is refused and shakes back, so a finished tiling has no gaps and no overlaps. "
"With 4 rows, every column takes 2 vertical tiles, and the grid is covered by 12 tiles. "
"The student repeats this on 4 rows and 7 columns, which takes 14 vertical tiles, and on 3 rows and 4 columns, where horizontal tiles fill each row, 2 to a row.\n\n"
"Next comes 5 rows and 7 columns. The student tries to tile it and always has one square left. They press Impossible and the tool asks for the number of squares. "
"They type 35 and the tool confirms that every tile covers 2 squares, so any number of tiles covers an even number, and 35 is odd. "
"A 3 by 5 grid, with 15 squares, is the same. If the student presses Impossible on a grid that can be tiled, the tool shows one way to tile it.\n\n"
"In the last card the student gives a strategy that works for every grid with an even number of rows, and tests it by changing the steppers: fill each column with vertical tiles. "
"The student also tries a grid with an even number of columns and an odd number of rows, and finds that rows of horizontal tiles work instead.\n\n"
"Hints come in order. First: how many squares does one tile cover? Then: count the squares: is the number even or odd? "
"Then: if the number of rows is even, fill each column with vertical tiles. "
"Last: an odd count can never be tiled; an even side always can, by columns or by rows. "
"Through doing, the student learns that a tiling has no gaps and no overlaps, that an odd number of squares can never be tiled with 2 by 1 tiles, and that an even side gives a plan for tiling the whole grid."
),

"M4.S22.T22.C61.2D1": (
"A 5 by 3 grid, with 5 rows and 3 columns, fills the screen. Tapping a square removes it. "
"A Colour button paints the grid like a chessboard with white corners, and two counters show how many black and white squares are left. "
"A tray holds 2 by 1 tiles and L-shaped tiles of three squares, which can be turned, and an Impossible button waits at the bottom.\n\n"
"In the first two cards the student removes the top right square and then the middle square of row 2, and tries to tile each remaining 14 squares. "
"Before trying, the student predicts: an even number of squares looks promising, but is it enough? "
"Both can be tiled with 7 tiles. With the top right corner removed, one horizontal tile fits in the top row and six vertical tiles fill the rest. "
"When the student presses Colour, the counters read 7 white and 7 black. Every 2 by 1 tile lands on one white and one black square, and the counters show this as tiles are placed.\n\n"
"In the third card the student removes the top middle square. However the tiles are placed, one square is left, "
"so the student presses Colour and the counters read 8 white and 6 black. Every tile covers one of each, so 7 tiles would need 7 of each, and Impossible is accepted with the counts shown. "
"In the fourth card the student looks for another square whose removal makes the grid impossible, and tries each black square, with 8 white and 6 black every time. "
"Pressing Impossible when the counts are equal is refused with a note to try harder to tile. "
"In the fifth card an 8 by 8 grid has two opposite corners removed. The student colours it and finds 32 of one colour and 30 of the other, so it cannot be tiled.\n\n"
"The last card gives a region of 12 squares in rows of 2, 2, 4 and 4. The student tiles it with four L-shaped tiles, turning them as needed. "
"The colour rule for 2 by 1 tiles does not apply here, as an L tile covers three squares, so the student must build the tiling.\n\n"
"Hints come in order. First: what colours does one 2 by 1 tile cover? Then: count black and white squares in the region. "
"Then: if the counts differ, no tiling is possible; if they are equal, try to build one. "
"Last: each tile covers one of each colour, so the counts must match before a tiling can exist. "
"Through doing, the student learns that colouring the squares turns a hard tiling question into counting, and that equal counts do not promise a tiling."
),
}
